# KNOWN GAP — REGRESSION introduced by R48 Track D2, BOTH BACKENDS, rc 134;
# `gg check` accepts. Filed as todo/t0908.
#
# One single-owner handle reaching TWO element slots — here the same Vector
# twice; the two-collections spelling (`a.push(m); b.push(m)`) behaves
# identically and is the same defect.
#
#   free(): double free detected in tcache 2                    (C and LLVM)
#   AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free in gorget_mutex_free  (--sanitize)
#
# ⚠ GREEN at the t0840 base blob 3b8a5561 (rc 0, prints `2`) — but green there
# only because the element slot held garbage that was never a real handle. Once
# the t0840 write repair put the handle in the slot, N slots means N owners.
#
# WHY THE ONE-SLOT REPAIR DOES NOT REACH IT. For a single slot the fix transfers
# the one owner to the collection and degrades the source to a borrow, so
# exactly one drop runs. That has no N-slot analogue: `elem_drop` is per-ARRAY,
# not per-element, so every slot holding the handle will drop it, and there is
# no lowering that gives one owner to N slots. `Mutex[T]`/`RWLock[T]` have no
# clone path, so there is nothing to put in slots 2..N either.
#
# ⛔ THIS IS THE OWNER ASK MADE CONCRETE, NOT A SEPARATE BUG. `AGENTS.md`
# § Ownership at Consuming Positions lists `Guard` — what `Mutex.lock()` returns
# — among the single-owner-by-design carve-outs that must emit
# `E_MoveWithoutOperator`, and does NOT list `Mutex` or `RWLock`. If they join
# that list this program is REJECTED and the gap closes; if they do not, they
# need a real clone path. Both are owner calls, and no lowering choice available
# to an executor makes an accepted N-slot program sound.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the wired test, under the accept resolution): prints `2`,
# rc 0, ASan-clean. Under the reject resolution the expectation flips to a
# check-time `E_MoveWithoutOperator` and this fixture moves to a reject test.

void main():
    Mutex[int] m = Mutex[int](5)
    Vector[Mutex[int]] all = []
    all.push(m)
    all.push(m)
    print(f"{all.len()}")
